WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Raw answer quality is only one selection dimension.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.410
A model that sometimes scores highly but frequently times out, returns an empty response,

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.070
violates the requested format,

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.640
or changes answers across runs may be a poor operational choice.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.351
Visual hallucination occurs when the model describes something not supported by the image.

00:00:16.351 --> 00:00:20.480
Counting errors may come from overlap, repeated patterns, or weak spatial tracking.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:25.615
OCR errors include substitutions, omissions, invented text, and incorrect reading order.

00:00:25.615 --> 00:00:29.245
Measure completion rate, latency distribution, memory failures, malformed output,

00:00:29.245 --> 00:00:31.320
and consistency across repeated runs.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.505
Three or more runs can reveal instability hidden by a single successful example.

00:00:35.505 --> 00:00:37.975
Keep generation settings fixed during comparison.

00:00:37.975 --> 00:00:40.895
Use a rubric that separates perception from explanation.

00:00:40.895 --> 00:00:42.930
Score whether visible facts were extracted correctly,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.175
then whether reasoning from those facts was sound.

00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:49.170
A polished answer should not receive credit for unsupported details.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.450
Operational reliability may favor a slightly lower-scoring model that is faster,

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.880
finishes consistently, and stays within memory.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.740
The right tradeoff depends on whether work is interactive, batch,

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.750
or near real time and how costly misses and false claims are.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.230
Test structured output if automation depends on it,

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.195
but retain human review for consequential decisions.

00:01:06.195 --> 00:01:10.020
Select using average quality, worst-case behavior, completion, and resource stability,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.920
not the single most impressive response.
